  <section class="faith-intro">
    <div class="container container-narrow">
      <details class="faith-intro-disclosure" data-faith-intro>
        <summary class="faith-intro-summary">
          <span class="faith-intro-summary-label" data-faith-intro-label>Read introduction</span>
          <span class="faith-intro-summary-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </summary>
        <div class="faith-intro-body">
          <p class="faith-intro-prose hero-excerpt-dropcap">The Westminster Shorter Catechism was finalized in 1647 by the Westminster Assembly, an English parliamentary body convened to bring the worship and doctrine of the Church of England into conformity with Reformed practice. Its 107 questions and answers were written for children and new converts, but its precision is such that adults still find it the surest summary of Reformed doctrine in English. It opens with one of the most-quoted lines in Protestant history: man’s chief end is to glorify God, and to enjoy him forever.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>
    
